  "question": {
    "text": "In Young's double-slit experiment, the distance between slits and the screen is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and monochromatic light of wavelength&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>600</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> is being used. A person standing near the slits is looking at the fringe pattern. When the separation between the slits is varied, the interference pattern disappears for a particular distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> between the slits. If the angular resolution of the eye is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac><mo>°</mo></math>, then the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> is close to",
